/// Something that happened, addressed to one person, waiting to be seen.
///
/// ─── Where this shape comes from ──────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// `@frontierjs/notifications` writes this model and does not ship it.
/// `drivers/inapp.ts` calls `asSystem().notification.create()` naming five
/// columns — `userId`, `type`, `data`, `contextType`, `contextId` — against a
/// hand-written structural type that is the only description of the shape
/// anywhere. `example/db/schema.lite` has the one instance. So the columns below
/// are not a proposal: five of them are already load-bearing, and an app that
/// spells one differently gets a runtime failure inside the driver.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `title` and no `body`.** A notification is an EVENT plus a payload, and
/// the words are rendered from `type` + `data` by the notification class at the
/// moment of display — which is what lets one stored row be an email in English,
/// a line in a dropdown, and a push in Spanish. Storing the sentence freezes all
/// three at write time and makes a copy change a migration.
///
/// **No `channel` or `sentAt`.** Delivery is a TRANSPORT concern and a
/// transport is not this row: the same notification fans out to in-app, email
/// and push, so a delivery column here would be one of N answers. `FJS-D06` —
/// in this framework a *channel* is junction's broadcast set, and the option
/// that carries a delivery medium is `transports:`.
///
/// **No `dismissedAt` beside `readAt`.** Read and dismissed is a product
/// decision, not a shape one; add it when a screen actually distinguishes them.
model Notification {
  id           Int       @id

  /// The recipient, as a plain column and NOT a relation — this file declares
  /// no identity model and cannot know yours. Wire it in your own schema:
  ///
  ///   user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  ///
  /// The type follows your `User`'s key. `example` uses `String` because auth
  /// mints uuids; an app on `Int @id` writes `Int`.
  ///
  /// The unit of address is a RECIPIENT rather than a user, and `id` is
  /// optional in the package for that reason — a shop customer with no account
  /// still gets an email. But a row keyed by an invented id is one nothing can
  /// ever read, so the in-app driver refuses an id-less recipient by name, and
  /// this column is therefore required.
  userId       String

  /// Which notification class this is. Not an enum: the set is declared in
  /// application code, one class per type, and an enum here would mean a
  /// migration every time somebody adds one.
  type         String

  /// The payload the class renders from. Everything the words need, captured at
  /// the moment of the event — so a notification about an order that has since
  /// been cancelled still reads correctly.
  data         Json

  /// Which row this is about — the polymorphic subject, as a type name and a
  /// key rather than a relation, because it points at a different model per
  /// row and a foreign key names one table.
  ///
  /// **Spelt `contextType`/`contextId` because the driver names those**, and
  /// `AuditEvent` in this same tree spells the identical idea
  /// `subjectType`/`subjectId`. The catalogue prefers `subject` for anything
  /// new; here the package has already chosen, and a rename is a migration plus
  /// a package change. See references/README.md.
  ///
  /// Nullable, both: a notification may be about nothing in particular.
  contextType  String?
  contextId    Int?

  /// Null until seen. A timestamp rather than a boolean so *when* survives, and
  /// because `readAt != null` reads the same as a flag anyway.
  readAt       DateTime?

  createdAt    DateTime  @default(now())

  /// Read 0 · create 8 · update 4 · delete 8.
  ///
  /// Read at 0 is not a hole — it is 0 BECAUSE the two policies below do the
  /// work, and a gate is the coarse floor. A stranger matches neither policy
  /// and gets an empty list with a 200.
  ///
  /// Create at 8 says no caller writes one: a notification is the application
  /// telling you something, and a person who can mint their own is a person who
  /// can mint one for somebody else. The driver uses `asSystem()`.
  ///
  /// Update at 4 is marking it read — the one write a recipient makes, narrowed
  /// to their own rows by the policy. Delete at 8 for the same reason as create.
  @@gate("0.8.4.8")
  @@allow('read',   userId == auth().id)
  @@allow('update', userId == auth().id)

  /// The list every screen asks for: this person's, newest first.
  @@index([userId, readAt])
}
